<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second International</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a>–<a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Socialist International</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second International, formally the Socialist International, was a federation of socialist and labour parties founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1889 and dominated by the Social Democratic Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It established International Workers' Day on 1 May, popularised the demand for an eight-hour day, expelled the anarchists and condemned revisionism at its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> Congress of 1904. From 1905 the prevention of war became its central task, and its congresses at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> and Basel called for working-class action against war. In 1914 its major parties rallied to their nations' war efforts and it collapsed, succeeded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Communist International</a> and the Labour and Socialist International.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-international.jpg" alt="Second International" width="330" height="289" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Congresso_Socialdem_1910.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Second International</b>, founded in Paris in July 1889, called for demonstrations for the eight-hour day on 1 May 1890, a date proposed by the American Federation of Labor: American unions had struck for it on 1 May 1886, days before the Haymarket affair in Chicago.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>After bitter disputes the Marxists expelled the Bakuninists at the Hague Congress of 1872, and the anarchists formed the St. Imier International; they were expelled from the <b>Second International</b> too, in 1896.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>The song's success went with that of the <b>Second International</b>, which adopted it as its official anthem, and by the 1910 International Socialist Congress in Copenhagen it had been sung in 18 languages.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-internationale">The Internationale</a></li></ul></section>
